{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## AC6 — the three drift failures have THREE different causes, measured from the run logs\n\nThe ticket was right to warn against one explanation absorbing all three. It does not. Each was\nread from the failing run's own log via `gh run view --log-failed`, not inferred.\n\n| date | run | failing step | exit | cause |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 09-03 | 33724312912 | `terraform plan (fail on drift)` | **2** | **GENUINE DRIFT** |\n| 09-04 | 33845247102 | `terraform fmt -check` | **3** | fmt aborted the job before the plan ran |\n| 09-05 | 33950205876 | `terraform plan` | **1** | the user_data overflow this ticket is about |\n\n**09-03 — genuine, unapplied drift.** Exit code 2, and the plan printed in full:\n\n```\nPlan: 0 to add, 11 to change, 0 to destroy.\n```\n\nEvery one of the 11 was a CloudWatch alarm moving `treat_missing_data = \"notBreaching\" ->\n\"breaching\"` (`voter_errors` and `# aws_cloudwatch_metric_alarm...` siblings are visible in the\nlog). That is committed HCL that had not been applied — exactly the condition this sweep exists\nto report, correctly reported. It is unrelated to the size overflow: `user_data.sh` was 12,325\nbytes that day.\n\n**09-04 — the sweep did not measure drift at all.** `terraform fmt -check -recursive` exited 3\non `monitoring_9ea3.tf`, and at that time `fmt` was still the FIRST STEP OF THE `plan` JOB, so\nit killed the job before `terraform plan` ran. This is bug `fd2b-7af3` reproducing verbatim —\na cosmetic whitespace difference blinding drift detection. It is already FIXED on today's tree:\n`fmt` is now its own job with no `needs:` relationship to `plan`, and the workflow's header\ncomment records why. So 09-04 needs no new work; it is evidence that the fix landed for the\nright reason.\n\n**09-05 — this ticket.** Exit 1, `expected length of user_data to be in the range (0 - 16384)`.\n\n## What this means beyond AC6\n\nThe three failures are one of each of the three outcomes the plan step could have, and the\nworkflow rendered all three as an identical bare red. That is precisely why AC5 is worth doing\nand why a human reading the sweep concluded \"drift\" for two days running. The fix names them.\n\n**Not established:** whether the 09-03 drift has since been applied. Today's plan against\n`origin/main` reports `Plan: 2 to add, 25 to change, 0 to destroy` — a larger pending set that\nincludes the 15 alarm fixes merged tonight as change 2683 — so a drift backlog is still open.\nThat backlog is an APPLY, which is an operator action; it is deliberately outside this change."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788647007487317001,"uuid":"5f37c674-2528-4da5-96a3-1179fb37ce10"}